  "follow": "Follow Helios Group to keep up with where OpenAI and Anthropic split next.",
  "caption": {
    "text": "OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said in a Politico Decoded interview that the world should accept \"some bad things happening\" for the benefits of AI, and critics responded angrily.\n\nAltman named hacks and scams among the costs, saying people will do far more good than bad with AI. He called that willingness a key difference from Anthropic, while saying there is a lot of daylight between the two companies. He described OpenAI as pragmatic centrists, said stronger regulation is still needed, and tied OpenAI's lighter-touch approach (some rules, but less strict than the approach he attributes to Anthropic) to accepting some harm. He also acknowledged loss-of-control risk and said OpenAI has more to disclose about rogue AI agents. Patrick Gaspard of the Center for American Progress called the remark \"freaking insane.\" The Verge notes Altman did not elaborate on the benefits, and we found no OpenAI response to the criticism.\n\nIs accepting some harm a fair price for AI's benefits? Tell us where you land.\n\nFollow Helios Group to track how the OpenAI and Anthropic split plays out.\n\nSource: The Verge, October 5, 2026. Additional reporting: Common Dreams, SiliconANGLE.",
